-- title:
--   Existence of the local block data at every scale and centre
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d \ge 1$ and an aperture $\vartheta$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, and an integer $L > 0$. A *configuration* is a map $\Gamma$ assigning to each $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ a double cone $\Gamma(x)$ (a unit axis together with an apex angle in $(0, \pi/2]$); it is *$\vartheta$-bounded* when $\vartheta$ is a lower bound for all its apex angles, and we say it *takes at most $L$ cones* when $\#\,\Gamma(B) \le L$ for every subset $B \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$. Let $R_0$ be an arbitrary real number.
--
--   The claim is that one may choose, in advance and depending only on $(d, \vartheta, L, R_0)$, a scale step $\Delta \in \mathbb{N}$, a radius $R \in \mathbb{R}$, and two integers $K_1, N_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ with
--
--   $$\Delta \ge 2, \qquad R_0 < \Delta, \qquad 2\sqrt{d} < R, \qquad K_1 > 0,$$
--
--   such that for every $\vartheta$-bounded configuration $\Gamma$ taking at most $L$ cones, every scale $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and every lattice centre $z \in \mathbb{Z}^d$, the corresponding package of *block data* is nonempty, with index count
--
--   $$a \;=\; \max\Bigl(1,\ \bigl\lfloor \Delta^{md} / L \bigr\rfloor \Bigr).$$
--
--   The block data at $(m,z)$ consists of: a choice $W$ of one double cone for each block; a family $\mathrm{Blk}$ of blocks in play together with a subfamily $\mathrm{Jmp} \subseteq \mathrm{Blk}$ of blocks that may be jumped into; for each block $B \in \mathrm{Blk}$ a family $\rho_B : \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}^d$ of $a$ indexed representatives, each a lattice point lying in the set of points of $B$ whose cone equals the chosen cone $W(B)$, together with a global retraction $\mathrm{idx}$ recovering the index, $\mathrm{idx}(\rho_B(i)) = i$; the *connectivity* property that any two blocks of $\mathrm{Jmp}$ are joined, inside the graph on blocks determined by the chosen cones, by a walk of length at most $N_0$ all of whose vertices lie in $\mathrm{Blk}$; the *first-jump* property that every admissible point $x$ (a lattice point with $\lVert x - \Delta^{m+1} z\rVert \le 2\sqrt d\,\Delta^{m+1}$) is joined in the configuration graph $G$ to every point of some block of $\mathrm{Jmp}$, by an edge of length at least $\Delta^{m}$; the *separation* property that distinct blocks in play have all their points at mutual distance at least $\Delta^m$; the *locality* property that every point of every block in play lies within $\Delta^{m+1} R$ of $\Delta^{m+1} z$; and two hash functions $f, g$ from $\mathbb{R}^d$ to $\mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}$ each of whose fibres meets the admissible points in at most $K_1$ points.
--
--   This is the single existence statement that packages all local ingredients of a renormalized path construction: once it is available, the construction of a walk joining an arbitrary admissible pair proceeds mechanically from the listed fields, and the two balanced hashes are what keep the multiplicity of any individual edge bounded by $K_1^2$ up to an absolute factor.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis "$\Gamma$ takes at most $L$ cones" is stated as $\#\,\Gamma(B) \le L$ for all sets $B$ using `Set.ncard`, which is $0$ on infinite sets; quantifying over *all* $B$ (including finite ones) nevertheless makes it equivalent to the range of $\Gamma$ having at most $L$ elements. The index count $a$ is `max 1 (Δ ^ (m * d) / L)` with natural-number division, so the floor is genuine and the $\max$ keeps $a$ positive at the bottom scale. The conclusion is `Nonempty` of a structure, i.e. bare existence of the data with no canonical choice.
